L1_1.mode = "text"
L1_1.title = "Ash in the Veins"
L1_1.author = "Diary Found on the Volcanic Island"
L1_1.text = "I have seen them. For a long time, I believed they were nothing more than stories told by those who had spent too many days at sea. Then I saw one with my own eyes. It was on the eastern coast, beyond the black rocks. We had followed the smoke, thinking we would find a camp. Marek, two other men from the village, and I. Instead, we found bodies. I do not know how many. Among them stood a man who was still alive. I remember Marek shouting something at him. There was no answer. Then the man rose from the ground. He did not jump. He did not climb. He rose. As though the air beneath his feet had become solid. Another one walked through the flames of a burning hut without being burned. His clothes caught fire for a few moments, but he kept walking. He did not seem to feel pain. Marek spoke that name. Sons of Decay. I had heard of them before. Men who survive where others die. Bodies that learn things a body should never know. I thought they were tales. I do not anymore. From that day on, I began studying Eteris. In our village, everyone drinks enough of it to remain stable. No one asks questions. When the amount in the blood begins to fall, the trembling starts. Then the fever. Then the dreams. I wanted to know what lay beyond. I began by slowly reducing the dose. The first night, nothing happened. The second, I woke on the floor. The third, I dreamed of the sea. Not the surface. The depths. I was beneath something immense. I could not see its shape, but I could feel it moving above me. When I woke, the veins in my arms had turned dark. For a few seconds, I could not feel the weight of my own body. I stood too quickly and struck the table. The table moved. Not slightly. It nearly crossed the entire room. I was afraid. I drank all the Eteris I had stored away. Within an hour, my veins returned to normal. So did my body. Stable. Perhaps too stable. Ever since, I have wondered what would have happened if I had not drunk it. No one in the village wants to speak of it. Old Edda claims Eteris does not make us stronger. She says it does the opposite. That it keeps something asleep. The others laugh at her. I no longer do. A few weeks ago, a wounded man arrived at the village. His side was torn open and his fever was high. We thought he would not survive the night. In his pack, I found papers, notes, and symbols I did not recognize. They spoke of Eteris. Not of how to find it. Of how to create it. I spent three nights copying those pages. At first, I thought they were the writings of a madman, but some of the substances were the same ones I had seen used by gatherers from the inner islands. Ashes. Organic matter. Something extracted from creatures that live where the land itself already seems dead. And one final step. That is what frightens me. I found everything I needed. I prepared the tools. One night, I even lit the fire. I was ready. But I did not do it. I do not know what that recipe truly produces. Perhaps Eteris. Perhaps something that merely resembles it. Perhaps this is how it begins. Perhaps those we now call Sons of Decay were once simply men sitting before a vessel, exactly as I was, convinced they could control what they had discovered. I put out the fire. I hid the recipe. I will not take it with me. It remains in the village, in the place where we kept the things that were never meant to be found by outsiders. If someone reads these words, it may still be there. But before searching for it, they should ask themselves why I was frightened enough to leave it behind. Tomorrow I will leave for the north. They say that beyond the mountains there are men capable of living for days without Eteris. They also say that some have died and returned from the sea changed. I do not know which story is true. Perhaps none of them. Perhaps all of them. I know only that something is changing inside us. Eteris slows it. Fear slows it. Perhaps even death slows it. But I do not believe anything can stop it. I do not want to die. But neither do I want to spend the rest of my life drinking something merely to remain fragile. If this diary is found and I do not return, do not search for my body. If I do return... I hope I am still myself."
