L1_1.mode = "text"
L1_1.title = "The Arena of the Isles"
L1_1.author = "Chronicle of Kharun"
L1_1.text = "In the beginning, we fought out of fear. Creatures descended from the forests, and the waters dragged beings onto our shores for which no one yet had a name. We learned to turn wood into spears, stone into blades, and our own hands into our final defense when every other weapon was lost. We thought we would stop fighting once the danger had passed. We were wrong. On days when the sea was calm and the forests were silent, men continued to challenge one another. Not out of hatred. Not for revenge. They wanted to know how far they could push themselves. Combat became a measure: of strength, of endurance, of the ability to fall and rise again having learned something from the blow that brought you down. The first challenge still remembered took place when men from two islands met upon the same shore. There were no creatures. There was no Velario. Only men armed with what they had built with their own hands. Instead of fighting over the land, they decided to fight over honor. The victor would return to his island with a portion of the other's resources. The defeated would keep his weapons, his scars, and the respect of his opponents. No one died for good that day. Yet no one forgot that battle. Others wanted to try. A circle of wood was built upon the shore. Fires were lit along its edges, and Eteris was kept ready for those who fell. Thus the Arena of the Isles was born. In time, the challenges became tournaments, and every community began wagering what it possessed: rare hides, minerals, herbs, weapons, and resources claimed from places from which many never returned. It was not war. It was a trial. In the Arena, we fight to the last blood, but not to the last life. Those who fall are brought back. Those who endure are remembered. Some claim that during the battles the sea becomes strangely still. Others swear they have seen something moving beneath the surface while hundreds of men shouted around the Arena. They say the Velario watches. That it hears iron striking iron, bones breaking, and bodies rising once again. I do not know whether it is true. But perhaps we fight for something more than victory. Perhaps every blow is our way of reminding the world that we are still here. That we can still fall. That we can still rise again. And that, despite everything this world has done to change us, we are still not afraid to be alive."
