L1_1.mode = "text"
L1_1.title = "The Six Ways of Combat"
L1_1.author = "Talen, Traveler Among the Isles"
L1_1.text = "I have walked among the islands long enough to see men fight out of fear, for glory, for revenge, and simply to survive. I once believed that, in the end, every fight was the same: one man facing another, with a weapon between them. I was wrong. Every land has developed its own way of fighting, and by watching them I came to understand that they are more than mere techniques. They are different ways of seeing the world. In the First Way, combat is control. Simple weapons, measured movements, no wasted strikes. Its warriors fight within a circle and salute their opponent before raising their weapon. Those who fall are helped back to their feet. To them, victory means preserving balance even when someone is trying to break it. In the Second Way, one fights to dominate. Heavy hammers, burning blades, blood and sparks. Their arenas are carved into stone, and the ground itself seems to burn beneath their feet. They do not wait for their opponent to make a mistake: they force one. To them, victory means imposing their will before the other can impose his own. The Third Way belongs to the altered forests. There are no arenas there, and rarely any spectators. They fight among the trees, in silence, with sudden attacks and movements so swift that the battle often ends before anyone realizes it has begun. To them, victory means never being where the enemy has chosen to strike. The Fourth Way was born among the ruins. Its warriors fight with improvised shields, patched armor, and weapons recovered from men who died long ago. Their duels are long and exhausting. They do not seek the perfect strike. They simply wait until the other no longer has the strength to raise his weapon. To them, victory means being the last one standing. The Fifth Way belongs to the sea. Its fighters face one another along the shores, often with their feet submerged in water. Their movements are fluid, and they rarely oppose force with force. They yield, deflect, fall, and rise again like waves against a cliff. To them, victory means adapting to what cannot be stopped. Of the Sixth Way, however, few are willing to speak. I have met only three men who claimed to have reached that land, and none of them would show me the way. They spoke of duels without spectators, slow and almost ritualistic battles in which two men might remain motionless for minutes before the first strike. They say that no one there fights to prove himself stronger. They fight to discover what remains of a man when fear, anger, and the desire for victory have been stripped away. For them, I do not know what it means to win. Perhaps there is no victory. After all these years, I have learned at least this: there is no single way to fight, just as there is no single way to survive. Perhaps that is precisely why, against all reason, we are still here."
