L1_1 = {}
L1_1.mode = "text"
L1_1.title = "The Three Ways of the Isles"
L1_1.author = "Arena Archive"
L1_1.text = "We are not all the same, and the Flooding did not make us any more alike. It merely made our differences impossible to ignore. When the old world vanished beneath the waters, those who remained chose three different ways to face what came after. The first built walls. They fortified the lands that were still habitable, protected the Eteris cauldrons, and turned every settlement into something capable of enduring the night, the creatures, and the sea. They believe stability is salvation, and they look with suspicion upon anyone willing to risk what remains in pursuit of what might exist. To them, the future means enduring. The second looked toward the horizon and refused to accept it as a boundary. They built boats from rotten wood, torn sails, and anything that could still float. Many left and never returned. Some came back with wounds no one knew how to heal. Very few returned carrying relics, maps, and tales of lands others believed had vanished. To them, the future cannot be found in the same place where the past died. The future lies beyond. Then there are those who build no empires and do not chase the horizon. They simply keep the fires burning. They distill Eteris, forge weapons, repair what others break, tend to the wounded, trade between hostile communities, and keep the Arenas open. They are called the Neutrals, although there is nothing neutral about keeping alive men who continue to fight one another. They seek no glory and are rarely remembered in the chronicles. Yet without them, neither of the other Ways would survive long enough to prove itself right. The islands are not divided by hatred. Hatred came later. The islands are divided by a much simpler question: when the world you knew disappears, do you preserve what remains, search for something new, or help others survive long enough to choose for themselves?"
