L1_1.mode = "text"
L1_1.title = "The Great Circle"
L1_1.author = "Central Arena Archive"
L1_1.text = "When the creatures withdrew for a season, men did not lay down their weapons. They sharpened them. Not to prepare for another war, but because after years spent surviving, no one knew who the better warrior was without something trying to devour them both. And so they built the Great Circle. They salvaged wood from shattered vessels, stone from submerged ruins, and rope from fishermen's nets. It was not beautiful. It was never meant to be. It was a circle large enough for two armed men to enter and small enough that neither could keep running forever. Fires were lit along its edges, and Eteris was prepared for those who would fall. The first fights offered no prizes. Men entered the Circle simply to discover who was worthy of remaining within it. Then men from the other islands arrived. They carried hand-forged weapons, shields marked with unknown symbols, and scars from battles no one else had witnessed. Challenges became tournaments. Resources were placed at stake. Rare hides, minerals, herbs, weapons, and even entire expeditions changed hands after a single fight. With rewards came rivalries. With rivalries came feuds. And from those feuds came alliances no treaty could ever have created. Blood became a language every island understood. It was the Neutrals who took control of the Great Circle. They registered the fighters, guarded the rewards, collected the wagers, and prevented challenges from ending in vengeance. They recorded the names of the victors because they understood before anyone else that a champion is not merely a man who knows how to fight. He is a symbol an entire island can follow. Even today, when thousands of voices shout the same name and iron rings within the Circle, some claim that even the sea grows quieter. Perhaps it listens. Perhaps it waits. We fight because this world never stops testing us. But within the Great Circle there remains one fundamental difference: here, we are the ones who choose when the trial begins."
