L1_1.mode = "text"
L1_1.title = "The Great Tournament of the Three Moons"
L1_1.author = "Chronicles of the Circle"
L1_1.text = "Every three moons, the islands come to a halt. Ships change course, workshops close before sunset, and men from lands that call one another enemies for the rest of the year dock at the same harbor. Banners are raised above the walls and the great fires of the Central Arena are lit. It is time for the Great Tournament of the Three Moons. Before the first blow is struck, the leaders of the three inhabited islands gather before the Arbiters. They do not sit upon thrones. Within the Circle, no one possesses one. They sit at the same height and publicly declare what they are willing to place at stake. Farmland, volcanic mines, access to harbors, routes considered safe, reserves of Eteris, outposts, and even prisoners captured during the wars between the islands. Nothing is too valuable when the alternative is a war capable of consuming hundreds of lives. This is why the Tournament is not merely about glory. It is judgment. A territorial dispute that would require months of bloodshed can end after only a few minutes within the Circle. A debt can be settled by a champion. An outpost can change banners without an army ever crossing the sea. Even the fate of prisoners can be entrusted to victory or defeat. The defeated are not necessarily killed. Some are returned. Others are ransomed. Others still become part of the price agreed upon before the Tournament begins. The Circle does not decide what is right. It only ensures that what was promised is honored. Each island therefore brings its finest fighters: men hardened by the sea, warriors raised in the lesser Arenas, and Sons willing to leave their gifts beyond the Seal and fight as men. During those days, the harbor becomes a different city. The crowds roar, wagers change hands with every blow, and merchants who arrived carrying a single crate leave wealthy enough to buy a ship. But when the final duel begins and the noise slowly fades, only two figures remain within the Circle. In that moment, there are no banners, factions, or titles. There are only two wills and everything that thousands of people have entrusted to them. Some swear that this is when the sea begins to move differently. Sailors claim to have seen an immense shadow passing slowly through the waters beneath the harbor during the most important fights. Perhaps the Velario watches. Perhaps it judges. Or perhaps it simply remembers something we continue to forget: even before the Flooding, men entrusted their fate to the strength of other men. Every three moons, the world of the islands changes. Not because of a storm. Not because of a creature. Not because of the sea's whim. It changes because someone enters the Circle, someone falls, and someone else remains standing."
