L1_1 = {}
L1_1.mode = "text"
L1_1.title = "The Tournament of the Storm"
L1_1.author = "Broken Chronicles"
L1_1.text = "Not every Three Moons is remembered with pride. There was one whose name, even today, is spoken only by archivists and by those who were young enough to survive that night. The three islands had gathered as they always did. That year, the stakes were higher than usual: a vast stretch of fertile land, the rights to a newly discovered mine, and dozens of prisoners captured during the clashes of the previous winter. The harbor had never held so many people. The wagers surpassed every figure ever recorded in the Archives, and the champions entered the Circle certain that their names would be remembered. In that, they were right. The final duel began at sunset and lasted far longer than expected. One of the two champions was a Son. He crossed the Seal without protest. He did not use his gift. He broke no law of the Circle. He fought as a man and, in the end, fell as a man. The spectators waited for him to rise. He did not. The Arbiters brought Eteris. It did nothing. For the first time since records had been kept, a Son had died within the Great Circle. No one celebrated the victory. For a few moments, thousands of people stood in complete silence. Then the wind changed. No clouds came and no rain fell, yet the harbor grew dark as though something had covered the sky. One by one, the torches along the docks went out. Then the water began to rise. Not as it does during a storm. No waves came from the distance. The entire sea itself was slowly rising against the walls, as though something beneath it were emerging. Dozens of witnesses later claimed to have seen an immense shadow moving beneath the ships. No one agreed on its shape. Everyone agreed on its size. The creatures came before dawn. They did not emerge from the forests. They came from the sea. The harbor burned as boats were shattered against the docks. Men, women, and fighters vanished beneath the water before they could even draw a weapon. The Great Tournament was suspended and, for the first time in the history of the Three Moons, warriors from every island fought side by side. There were no territories to claim that night. There were no debts, prisoners, or banners. There was only something trying to get in, and men who refused to let it pass. When morning came, the sea withdrew. No one knew why. Since then, the Three Moons have been observed differently. The rules are stricter. The Arbiters personally inspect every Seal. Wagers are limited, and part of the guard always remains facing the harbor, even during the final duel. No one has ever proven that the death of the Son and what happened that night were connected. The oldest archivists still insist it was merely a coincidence. And yet, whenever a Son crosses the Seal and steps into the Circle, at least one man in the crowd stops watching the fight. He watches the sea. He does not fear what the Son might do. He fears what might happen if the Son does not rise again."
