L1_1.title = "The Order of the New World"
L1_1.author = "Council of the Founders"
L1_1.text = "When the waters rose, there were no nations, borders, or kingdoms left. There were only fragments of land and lost men upon the shores of islands that often did not even have names. It became necessary to choose whether to let fear finish what the sea had begun, or to bring order to what remained. The first Founders gathered not as kings, but as survivors. They decided that every habitable land would have a protected harbor, a cauldron for the distillation of Eteris, a stretch of cultivable land that no dispute would be allowed to destroy, and a place where men could learn to fight. Survival would come before pride. Whoever defended an island would defend all who lived upon it. Whoever willingly endangered it would answer before the Circle. But men did not share the same vision of the future. Some chose to protect what remained and became the Wardens of the Anchor. Others looked toward the sea and decided that no horizon would define the limits of their knowledge: they became known as the Seekers of the Horizon. Others still rejected both paths. They kept the fires burning, distilled Eteris, raised horses, forged weapons, tended the wounded, and kept open the places where even enemies could meet without beginning a war. They became the Neutrals. It was decreed that great disputes would not be allowed to consume entire generations. Whenever possible, territories, mines, routes, and debts would be entrusted to the Circle, and chosen champions would carry the fate of their communities upon their shoulders. Neutral Arbiters would safeguard the rules so that combat remained a measure rather than becoming chaos. Even the sea was given a law: no one would risk the survival of an entire island to satisfy personal ambition. Expeditions would require supplies, trained men, and vessels capable of returning. The horizon could be sought, but never underestimated. Thus the Order of the New World was born. Not from the wealth of the past, nor from its laws now drowned beneath the waters, but from four necessities older than any kingdom: to build, to cooperate, to fight, and to know one's limits. As long as men remain capable of these things, as long as every three moons they can still gather within the Circle and accept the outcome of the trial, the world will not have ended. It will merely have changed its form."
