L1_1.title = "The Uncarved Treaty"
L1_1.author = "Seal of the Founders"
L1_1.text = "This document shall not be read within the Circle, nor carved upon the public tablets. Some truths strengthen a people. Others divide them before they are ready to face them. The order of the islands depends not only upon what men know, but upon the balance between what they know and what they fear. We have observed the creature of the sea long enough to recognize behaviors incompatible with the image of it that we preserve publicly. It could destroy our vessels with ease. Rarely does it do so. Hulls recovered beyond the currents show no bite marks and no signs of attempts to feed. They show fractures caused by precise impacts, often sufficient to prevent further travel without destroying those aboard. This observation must not be made public. Fear of the sea remains a necessary deterrent. It has also been confirmed that beyond a certain distance, the currents change in nature. The water takes on properties that none of our scholars can explain, and expeditions that crossed that boundary left behind no wreckage, bodies, or signals. No expedition beyond the extreme boundary shall be authorized without unanimous consent. Regarding those individuals known as Sons, we possess insufficient evidence to consider them saviors, nor can we define them as an absolute threat. Their emergence appears connected to phenomena that began before our communities were formed. They may live among men, fight for the islands, and sit upon their councils, but no Son shall ever possess alone the authority required to lead an entire population beyond a point from which there may be no return. There remains one final consideration that none of us wishes to put into writing, and yet for that very reason it must be preserved. A people without a common danger quickly finds new enemies among its neighbors. The Shadow in the Sea shall therefore continue to be publicly described as both threat and boundary. As long as men fear what exists beyond the islands, perhaps they will hesitate before destroying one another over what exists upon them. But if one day the creature should die or disappear and the waters do not recede, then every Founder still alive must consider a possibility that none of us would dare speak before the people today: the creature may not be the cause of our imprisonment. It may be part of it. It may even be what keeps something worse outside. If that day comes, do not call it victory. Consider it the end of an equilibrium whose rules we never understood. This treaty bears no names. Only seals. Because some decisions carry enough weight to crush even those who made them."
