L1_1.title = "The Sobriety of the Isles"
L1_1.author = "Council of the Three Lands"
L1_1.text = "The old world loved excess. It built towers higher than necessity demanded, amassed wealth no man could ever consume, and created things meant to be displayed before they were ever meant to be used. When the waters rose, they did not merely take the cities. They took the illusion of abundance. It was not only houses that fell, but the belief that mankind was untouchable. It was not only metal that sank, but the conviction that all growth could continue forever. When nothing remained but islands, wood, stone, and hands calloused enough to work them, we understood that the world had not been reduced to poverty. It had been reduced to what was essential. In the new world, we do not build to impress, but to endure. We do not forge so that something may shine, but so that it may last. We do not raise animals for luxury, but so that men and animals may survive together. Those who hoard far beyond what they need are regarded with suspicion, not because possession is a sin, but because we have already seen where a world incapable of recognizing when it has enough will lead. Even the Sons of Decay are misunderstood by those who continue to think according to the laws of the past. They do not survive because they possess more than others. They survive because they have accepted losing something. They have understood, for better or worse, that Decay does not preserve: it transforms. The wealth of the islands is not gold. It is a horse tended before it falls, a boat repaired before the storm, a blade sharpened before nightfall, Eteris shared with those who have less, and the ability to fight when necessary and stop when it is no longer needed. If one day we return to desiring useless towers and power without measure, the sea will not need to drown us a second time. Ruin is born within man long before it becomes visible in the world around him. As long as we know how to recognize the limit, as long as the Circle remains a measure rather than a spectacle, and what we build has a purpose before it has value, these islands will continue to exist. Not as what remains of the old world. But as the choice not to repeat what destroyed it."
