L1_1.title = "The Merchant of Towers"
L1_1.author = "Chronicles of the Broken Isle"
L1_1.text = "He came from the sea with crates filled with polished metal, unbroken glass, and fabrics in colors many children of the islands had never seen. He spoke constantly of the greatness that had been lost. He said the Flooding had been a tragedy, not a lesson, and that men would prove they had overcome the old world's destruction only when they began building something greater once again. Many laughed. Some followed him. He began raising a tower using rare wood, volcanic stone, and enough metal to reinforce three harbors. Each week it rose higher above the houses surrounding it. When it was finished, it reflected the sun and could be seen far beyond the coast. The Merchant pointed toward it and said that mankind had finally begun looking upward again. For several days, nothing happened. Then, on a moonless night, fishermen noticed unusual movement near the coast. Creatures no one had ever seen so close to the settlements began emerging from the water. Some said they were drawn to the tower's reflections. Others claimed something within the metal produced a sound only they could perceive. No one could prove either theory. The storm arrived the following night. The tower collapsed before dawn, taking with it two storehouses and supplies that could have sustained the island for months. The Merchant survived. No one beat him. No one confiscated what remained of his crates. When he returned to the shore, he simply found the men of the island waiting for him in silence. They pointed toward his boat. He left that morning. Since then, no structure on the Broken Isle has risen higher than its purpose requires. Some call it superstition. Perhaps it is. But no one has seen those creatures near the coast since."
