L1_1.title = "Of the Lost Cities"
L1_1.author = "Recovered Testimony"
L1_1.text = "The cities of the old world were tall, bright, and incapable of knowing silence. Men walked among towers of glass and steel, convinced that nothing could threaten them anymore. They had carved through mountains, bent rivers to their will, illuminated the night, and built machines capable of running without horses. Food was so abundant that it was thrown away. Water reached their homes without anyone having to search for it. Entire buildings were raised not because anyone needed to live within them, but so that others could look upon them. They called all of this progress. And they believed progress could never end. When the sea began to rise, they said it would fall again. When the storms grew stronger, they called them anomalies. When entire coastlines disappeared, they built larger walls and continued as before. Every warning was met with the same answer: we will find a solution tomorrow. Then came a day when there was no longer a tomorrow distant enough. Now those cities lie beneath the water. Towers that once dominated the sky are covered in algae, and nameless creatures pass through rooms where men dead for generations once argued about the future. I do not know whether it was the sea that destroyed the old world. Perhaps it merely dealt the final blow. Whoever finds these words, remember this: no civilization understands that it has grown too large until something reminds it how small it truly is. We have chosen to remember that every day."
