L1_1.title = "The Rite of the Threshold"
L1_1.author = "Transcribed Oral Tradition"
L1_1.text = "There is no formula capable of transforming a man into what we now call a Son. Anyone who claims otherwise is selling superstition or seeking followers. There is, however, a moment that many of those who have changed describe in the same way. They call it the Threshold. It cannot be imposed. It can only be crossed. Those who feel themselves drawing near begin by depriving themselves not of what is necessary, but of what is excessive. They slowly reduce their Eteris without abandoning it. They watch the sea without attempting to dominate it. They fight without hatred. They give part of their supplies to those who have less and willingly surrender something they could have kept. These are not sacrifices offered to a deity. They serve to free a man from the things that continue to convince him that survival means holding on. In some communities, the rite ends upon the shore. A circle is drawn in the sand using ash and seawater. The one seeking the Threshold sits at its center and remains silent. No words are recited. Nothing from the sea is summoned. He must simply listen until his own heartbeat seems to merge with the rhythm of the waves. Almost always, nothing happens. Some speak of deep dreams. Others describe a vibration within their bones, as though something had answered from very far away. Many rise exactly as they sat down. But on rare occasions, someone returns changed. Sometimes the change does not appear in the body until days later. The eyes, however, change immediately. Once, I witnessed a man return from the Threshold. When I asked what he had seen, he answered: \"Nothing. I stopped looking.\" I did not understand then. Perhaps I still do not. Remember, however, what the oldest among us repeat: the Threshold does not grant power. Power is merely a consequence. What is sought is accord. Those who reach it wishing to dominate Decay rarely return better than they were. Some do not return at all."
