L1_1.title = "Still Waiting"
L1_1.author = "Elyra\226\128\153s Diary"
L1_1.text = "Seventeen days have passed since his boat left the harbor. I keep counting them even though no one asks me anymore when he will return. At first, everyone did. They told me the currents could delay an expedition, that perhaps they had found land, that the Seekers would return carrying things we had not seen in years. I smiled and pretended to believe them. Every evening, I left a lantern burning by the window so he could recognize our home from the sea. Now I think I light it only for myself. Thirty-two days have passed. Today they stopped searching for them. No wreckage. No bodies. Not a single piece of the ship carried back by the currents. It is as though the sea erased them. A man at the harbor told me I should at least be grateful that I had not seen my husband's body. I wanted to strike him. A body means someone has died. Absence means continuing to imagine where they might be. Forty-six days have passed. This morning, one of the sailors returned. He was not part of their ship. He had left several days later with a second expedition. They found him clinging to a piece of wood near the boundary of the currents. He refuses to speak to anyone. When I told him my husband's name, he began to cry. Then he grabbed my arm and told me something I cannot forget: \"It does not protect us from the sea. It protects us from what is out there.\" I do not know what he meant. Fifty-one days have passed. I went to see him again. This time he spoke. Not much. He said that beyond the boundary there are things that should not exist, and that when the Shadow approaches, those things move away. I asked him whether the Velario was protecting us. He laughed. I will never forget that laugh. He told me: \"Protecting does not mean loving.\" Since then, I have not been able to stop thinking about his words. We protect crops because we want to eat. We protect horses because we need them to run. We protect the fire because without it, the cold comes. What if we, too, are being protected for a reason? Sixty days have passed. My husband is not coming back. I can finally write it. He is not coming back. I do not know what lies beyond those waters, and I no longer want to know. But now I fear even more what remains here with us. Perhaps we have been asking the wrong question all these years. We keep asking why the Velario will not allow us to leave the islands. No one seems to ask why it is so interested in keeping us alive. If you find this diary, listen to me. Do not search for my husband. Do not search for that expedition. Do not cross the boundary to discover the truth. This place is cursed. Perhaps the sea protects us from something worse, but I have begun to believe that protection comes at a price we do not yet understand. Protect the people who remain. Protect the children. Protect what remains of our kind. Not because these islands are our home, but because we may be the last human beings left in the world. And if someone, or something, truly is keeping us here for some greater purpose, pray that the day it needs us never comes. I put out the lantern."
