L1_1.mode = "text"
L1_1.title = "Those Who Took to the Sea"
L1_1.author = "Unknown Author"
L1_1.text = "It is not true that no one has ever tried to leave the island. Many have tried. The first I remember built a boat from the timber of abandoned houses along the coast. There were seven of them. They carried water, ropes and enough food for many days. They left at dawn. We watched them until their sail became a point on the horizon. Then they disappeared. Three days later, the first body returned to the shore. The second came that same evening. The others were returned by the sea over the following days. The boat was never found. We blamed a storm. <<PAGE>> [ THE OTHERS ] So others tried. They chose a night without wind and built a stronger vessel. They carried no sails. They said they would row until the island disappeared behind them. One of those men returned alive. We found him at dawn, lying where the water met the sand. He had no wounds. He remembered nothing of the journey. He only kept repeating that throughout the night, no matter which direction they rowed, they could always see the island ahead of them. He died before sunset. <<PAGE>> [ EVERY ATTEMPT ] After them came many more. Some left alone. Others departed in groups large enough to fill entire boats. There were those who followed the coastline for days before turning toward the open sea, convinced there had to be a place where the passage was possible. The result never changed. Sometimes pieces of the boats returned. Sometimes the bodies returned. Sometimes nothing returned at all. Years passed, and still someone would eventually convince himself that all the others had simply made a mistake. That he had understood something they had not. The sea proved every one of them wrong. <<PAGE>> [ THE LAST TWELVE ] The last attempt happened many years ago. There were twelve of them. Before leaving, they promised that if they found something beyond the horizon, they would light a great fire during the night so that we could see it from the shore. That evening, many of us waited along the coast. No light appeared beyond the horizon. Instead, twelve small blue lights appeared upon the beach. One beside another, exactly where the waves reached the sand. At dawn, we found their bodies. All twelve. No boat. No wounds that could explain their deaths. Only their bodies, wet with seawater, as though they had never traveled farther than the shore. <<PAGE>> [ THE LESSON ] Since then, we still build boats. We fish. We travel along the coast and between the nearby islands. But when the sea becomes too wide and the land begins to disappear behind us, we turn back. Not because anyone forbids us to continue. Not because we know what waits beyond that point. We turn back because too many before us did not. Some believe there is something beneath the water. Others say the currents themselves lead every vessel back. A few believe the island is responsible. I do not know which of them is right. I know only this: the island is not difficult to leave. Something simply refuses to let us go."
